textosLlegan("theory", {"fil-t1":{"html":"<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>How to study this topic: read each section slowly, underline the <strong>terms in bold</strong> (they are the vocabulary of the exam) and, at the end of each block, check whether you could explain it in your own words. The two comparison tables and the sample questions are the best guide for revision.</p> </blockquote>\n<p><strong>Being born unfinished: helplessness as the starting point.</strong> A foal stands up within a few hours of being born; a human baby takes around a year to take its first steps and many more to manage on its own. We are born unfinished, and biology calls that long childhood <strong>neoteny</strong>, in which we keep juvenile traits and depend on the care of others. But that lack has another side. Since almost nothing is already settled for us by instinct, we have to learn everything, and that is why we ask: first “What is that?”, then “Why?” and, sooner or later, “Who am I?”. Aristotle opens his <em>Metaphysics</em> (I, 1, 980a) by saying that all human beings desire by nature to know. That desire is born, in part, from what we lack.</p><p>Such a being cannot be limited to being <strong>trained</strong>. Training is fixing a behaviour through repetition and reward, as when a dog is taught to sit in exchange for a biscuit. <strong>Educating</strong> is something else: forming judgement, the capacity to ask and to decide for oneself. Learning to use a kitchen knife without cutting yourself is training, and it is necessary; thinking about when it is right to use it, for what and against what, is already education. Kant argued in <em>On Education</em> (1803) that the human being only becomes human through education, and he warned that it is not enough to train a child like an animal: what is decisive is that the child learns to think.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: imagine a person who has only been trained and another who has been educated, faced with a new situation that no one taught them. What would each one do? Which of the two would be more likely to get it right, and why?</p>\n<h2>1. What is philosophy?</h2>\n<p><strong>Knowing is not the same as saber.</strong> In everyday language we use the two verbs almost as synonyms, but it is worth distinguishing them. <strong>Knowing</strong> is the concrete act of grasping an object: recognising that this is a triangle, that the noise at the window is the rain or that the person entering the classroom is your desk mate. <strong>saber</strong>, by contrast, is the result of a whole series of operations (observing, comparing, demonstrating, ordering) through which we take possession of an area of reality and turn it into a system. You know your classmate; you know, or are coming to know, geometry. You can know a particular recipe for marmitako and follow it step by step without knowing how to cook: someone who knows how to cook understands why the onion is fried first and could improvise with whatever is in the fridge.</p><p>They are, therefore, related, but not identical. One can know an isolated fact without knowing anything of what explains it, whereas saber brings together many pieces of knowledge and links them with reasons. Plato saw this clearly in the <em>Meno</em> (97a-98a): a true opinion (<em>dóxa</em>) is useful while it lasts, but it escapes like the statues of Daedalus, which according to legend ran away if they were not tied down; it only becomes knowledge (<em>epistḗmē</em>) when we tie it down with reasoning about its cause. Aristotle will say something similar in the <em>Metaphysics</em> (I, 1): the person with experience knows that something happens; the person who truly knows also knows why it happens, that is, its causes.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: can you know something without ever having known it directly, like the distance from the Earth to the Moon? And can you know something without knowing anything about it?</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t1-3\">The word <strong>philosophy</strong> comes from Greek and is made up of two roots: <strong>philía</strong> (<em>love</em>, <em>friendship</em>, <em>desire for something</em>) and <strong>sophía</strong> (<em>wisdom</em>). Philosophy therefore means <strong>love of wisdom</strong>. This etymology is already an important clue: the philosopher is not someone who possesses the truth and hands it out, but someone who <strong>desires</strong> and <strong>seeks</strong> it. That is why Pythagoras, to whom the invention of the word is attributed, preferred to call himself a “lover of knowledge” rather than a “wise man”. This anecdote, however, is a late tradition: it comes from Heraclides Ponticus (4th century BC) and has reached us through Cicero and Diogenes Laertius.</p>\n<p><strong>Looking or touching? The sense that guides knowledge.</strong> Each philosopher has, without saying so, a favourite sense, and that sense shapes their idea of what knowing is. Plato chooses <strong>sight</strong>. In the allegory of the cave (<em>Republic</em> VII, 514a-518d), the prisoners see only shadows, and knowing is gradually coming out into the light until one can look at the things themselves. From this he derives an idea of education: educating is not putting knowledge into a blind soul, as one might put sight into eyes that do not have it, but turning the whole soul towards what truly is (518b-d). For Plato, learning is learning to look beyond appearances.</p><p>Aristotle also praises sight: at the beginning of the <em>Metaphysics</em> (I, 1) he says that it is the sense we value most, because it shows us many differences among things. But in <em>On the Soul</em> (II-III) he grants <strong>touch</strong> a more basic place: it is the sense all animals have, without which none could live, and it is not located in a single organ but spread throughout the body. He adds something surprising: the human being surpasses the other animals in fineness of touch, and Aristotle links this with its intelligence (II, 9).</p><p>Think of a piece of fruit at the market: from its colour you can suspect that it is ripe, but only by feeling it do you confirm it. Plato thinks of knowledge as contemplation at a distance; Aristotle, as contact with things, which starts from the senses and does not abandon them.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: what important information does only touch give you, and what does only sight? What kind of school would resemble each of the two models?</p>\n<p>From this it follows that philosophy has two faces. It is, on the one hand, an <strong>attitude</strong> towards life: that of someone who takes nothing for granted, asks questions and wants to truly understand. And it is, on the other hand, a <strong>body of knowledge</strong>: the set of reflections, theories and problems that men and women have been developing throughout history on the great questions.</p>\n<p><strong>Wonder and curiosity.</strong> Already in Antiquity, Plato and Aristotle pointed out that philosophy is born from <strong>wonder</strong>: the capacity to be struck by what others find obvious. A child asks why the sky is blue or what happens when we die; the adult has often stopped asking. Philosophising is, in part, recovering that sense of strangeness. For Aristotle, moreover, wonder is the origin of the search for <strong>causes</strong>: whoever wonders asks <em>why</em>. Alongside wonder there act <strong>curiosity</strong> (the urge to investigate and learn, which we also share with other animals) and <strong>doubt</strong> (being aware that we do not know and wanting to know).</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>“Human beings begin and have always begun to philosophise moved by wonder.” — Aristotle, <em>Metaphysics</em></p> </blockquote>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t1-7\"><strong>“I know only that I know nothing.”</strong> This sentence, which tradition attributes to <strong>Socrates</strong> (in Plato’s <em>Apology</em> he says, more exactly, that he does not think he knows what he does not know), sums up that attitude. Recognising one’s own ignorance is not giving up, quite the opposite: it is the first step towards learning. Whoever thinks they already know everything seeks nothing; whoever knows they do not know, investigates.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>“I know only that I know nothing.” — attributed to Socrates</p> </blockquote>\n<p><strong>To read the source.</strong> The two texts in this section are in the reading <a href=\"#lecturas/fil-lec-platon-aristoteles\">‘Plato and Aristotle: why do we start to philosophise?’</a>. In the first, Socrates, puzzled by the oracle of Delphi, questions politicians, poets and craftsmen and discovers that his only advantage is that he does not believe he knows what he does not know; in the second, Aristotle explains that we philosophise out of wonder and that this is why philosophy is the only ‘free’ science, the one sought for its own sake.</p>\n<p><strong>The questions of philosophy.</strong> Not all questions are philosophical. It is worth distinguishing three types:</p>\n<ul> <li>Questions of <strong>taste or personal opinion</strong>: the answer depends on each person. For example: <em>do I prefer the potato omelette pintxo or the cod one?</em> One can explain one’s reasons, but there is no room for a rational debate <strong>like the one that takes place</strong> in science or philosophy.</li> <li><strong>Scientific</strong> questions: they are settled by observing, measuring or experimenting. For example: <em>how many bones does an adult have?</em> or <em>how long does sunlight take to reach the Earth?</em></li> <li><strong>Philosophical</strong> questions: no single piece of data settles them, nor are they a matter of taste. They are discussed with <strong>arguments for and against</strong> and point to the ultimate foundations of things. For example: <em>am I still the same person I was at the age of five?</em>, <em>is it fair that some are born rich and others poor?</em>, <em>should I tell a friend a truth that will hurt them?</em> or <em>what makes a life worth living?</em></li> </ul>\n<p>There is a fourth case that should not be confused with these: <strong>mathematical</strong> or <strong>logical</strong> questions (<em>is the number 97 prime?</em>). They are not settled by observing the world either, but they are not philosophical, because they are closed by a <strong>proof</strong> that starts from accepted definitions and axioms. The philosophical question, by contrast, can be settled neither by an experiment nor by a calculation: it can rely only on the quality of the arguments. That is why philosophising is difficult and requires learning and practice.</p>\n<p>Learning to recognise and formulate a good philosophical question is already, in itself, an exercise in philosophy.</p>\n<h2>2. From myth to logos</h2>\n<p>Philosophy was born in <strong>Greece, around the 6th century BC</strong>. Before it, ancient peoples explained the world through <strong>myths</strong>. That is why the birth of philosophy is described as the <strong>step “from myth to logos”</strong>: the moment when human beings stopped settling for inherited beliefs and began to use their best capacity, <strong>reason</strong> (<em>logos</em> means in Greek both “word” and “reason”; <em>mythos</em>, by contrast, means “story” or “tale”).</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t1-15\"><strong>What is myth?</strong> Myths are <strong>traditional stories</strong>, transmitted <strong>orally</strong>, which tell of extraordinary events starring supernatural beings: gods, demigods, heroes and fantastic creatures. With them, ancient peoples explained what they could not otherwise understand: lightning and thunder, earthquakes, illnesses or the origin of the universe. Mythical thought has several characteristic features:</p>\n<ul> <li>It is <strong>anthropomorphic</strong>: it attributes human physical features and emotions (anger, envy, whim) to the forces of nature and to the gods.</li> <li>It is <strong>animist</strong>: it believes that spirits with intelligence, feelings and intentions dwell in nature (a forest, a river or a tree would be “animated”).</li> <li>It is <strong>indeterminist or arbitrary</strong>: it thinks that things happen according to the capricious will of the gods, and not through regular causes and laws. Thunder sounds “because the gods are angry”.</li> <li>It is <strong>normative</strong>: it does not only explain the world, it also dictates how to behave, through rites, prohibitions and taboos.</li> <li>It is <strong>uncritical and dogmatic</strong>: this knowledge is believed to have been revealed by the gods, so it is accepted without discussion. It is often guarded by a privileged caste (priests, magicians, shamans) that keeps a <strong>secret</strong>, non-public knowledge linked to <strong>magic</strong>.</li> </ul>\n<p><strong>Sacred story and rite.</strong> Those features describe myth from the outside, by comparing it with science. People who study the myths of living peoples add something the list does not include: myth is a <strong>sacred</strong> story, and often it is not told at just any time, but at a festival or a ceremony, bound up with a <strong>rite</strong>. To tell whether a story is a myth and not a folk tale, the Finnish folklorist <strong>Lauri Honko</strong> proposed in 1972 looking at four things at once:</p>\n<ul> <li><strong>Form</strong>: it is a narrative, a story with characters and events, not a law or a prayer.</li> <li><strong>Content</strong>: it tells how the world, human beings or a custom began, in a primordial time, different from ours.</li> <li><strong>Function</strong>: it serves as a model; it explains and justifies why things are done today the way they are done.</li> <li><strong>Context</strong>: it is recited or performed in a rite; when the rite disappears, the story may go on being told, but by then as a legend or a folk tale.</li> </ul>\n<p>An example close to home: according to a story collected by <strong>José Miguel de Barandiarán</strong>, the ancient Basques, frightened by the beings of the night, asked for help; first they were given the moon, then the sun and, finally, the <strong>eguzkilore</strong>, the flower that imitates the sun and is still nailed to the door of some farmhouses. It has the form of a narrative, speaks of an origin and justifies a practice; if we see it nailed to a door, we know that for someone it is still fulfilling its function. That is why myth is <strong>true for those who live it</strong>: they do not examine it like a journalist asking whether an event happened or not, but inhabit it, just as someone celebrating a festival does not ask whether it is ‘real’. The pair ‘true or made up’ is ours; myth answers a different question, that of how we should live and why the world is ordered as it is. Philosophy is not born from denying it all value, but from asking of it what myth does not give: <strong>reasons</strong> that anyone can examine.</p>\n<p><strong>The step to rational explanation (logos).</strong> With the first Greek philosophers another way of thinking appears. <strong>Thales of Miletus</strong> (c. 624–546 BC), considered the first philosopher, wanted to explain nature <strong>without resorting to the gods</strong>. He asked about the <strong>arkhé</strong>: the principle or common element from which everything proceeds (for Thales, that principle was water). It matters less whether he was right or not; what is decisive is the change of method. Rational explanation seeks <strong>natural causes</strong>, relies on <strong>arguments</strong> (not on tradition or authority), has a known <strong>author</strong> (it stops being anonymous) and, above all, is <strong>critical</strong>: every conclusion can be discussed, checked and improved.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr> <th></th> <th><strong>Myth</strong></th> <th><strong>Logos (philosophy)</strong></th> </tr> </thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr> <td>Who or what explains the world?</td> <td>The gods and supernatural beings, with capricious wills</td> <td>Natural causes (the <em>physis</em>, the <em>arkhé</em>) and rational principles</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Type of explanation</td> <td>Fantastic, anthropomorphic and animist story</td> <td>Explanation through reasons and arguments</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>How is it transmitted?</td> <td>By oral tradition, from generation to generation (poets, priests)</td> <td>In writing and in public debate; with a known author</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Can it be discussed or checked?</td> <td>No: it is dogmatic, accepted without criticism</td> <td>Yes: every conclusion can be examined, criticised and revised</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Attitude towards knowledge</td> <td>Uncritical and conformist</td> <td>Critical and open</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Example</td> <td>Thunder sounds because the gods are angry</td> <td>Thunder is explained by physical causes that we can investigate</td> </tr> </tbody> </table>\n<p><strong>A qualification (Hegel).</strong> Centuries later, the German philosopher <a href=\"#biografias/hegel\">Hegel</a> insisted that myth was not a simple error: the content of myth is already thought (this is a paraphrase, not a literal quotation, of the Introduction to his <em>Lectures on the History of Philosophy</em>). What changes with philosophy is not so much what is said as the <strong>form</strong> of saying it: instead of stories and images, concepts and reasons. That is why the step from myth to logos is a <strong>sublation</strong> that preserves what is valuable in myth, not a leap from lies to truth.</p>\n<p><strong>Thinking with concepts: abstraction.</strong> What Hegel calls ‘concepts’ deserves an explanation, because without them there would be no philosophy. To <strong>abstract</strong> (from the Latin <em>abstrahere</em>, ‘to separate’, ‘to draw out of’) is to notice what several things have in common and to set aside what distinguishes them. Think of Lur, the dog that belongs to your group of friends in the village, an Ertzaintza German shepherd and a neighbour’s chihuahua: they are not alike in size, colour or temperament, and yet all three are <em>dogs</em>. The result of that exercise is a <strong>concept</strong>: a mental representation that has no size or colour and that holds for any dog, including the ones you will never see. And you can go up a step: dog, mammal, living being, being. Each step covers more things and says less about each of them.</p>\n<p><strong>What the mind separates: <em>aphaíresis</em>.</strong> Aristotle called this operation <em>aphaíresis</em>, ‘to remove’, ‘to separate’, and the Latin word <em>abstractio</em> is its translation. His favourite example is that of the mathematician. A ball, an orange and a drop of water are spherical, but the geometer does not study the rubber, the peel or the water: he considers the sphere by separating it in thought from matter and from movement, even though outside the mind no sphere exists without matter (<em>Physics</em> II, 2, 193b31–35). That is why he calls the objects of mathematics ‘what is said by abstraction’ (<em>On the Soul</em> III, 7, 431b12–16). To abstract is not, then, to invent: it is to focus on one aspect of something real and leave the others aside. The result, however, is not like a photograph. An image is always of something concrete: if you imagine a triangle, you see it as equilateral or scalene, large or small. John Locke spoke of a general idea of a triangle that was none of these, and George Berkeley replied that he was unable to imagine any such thing (<em>Principles of Human Knowledge</em>, Introduction, § 13, 1710). Descartes had already given the key: we can think with complete clarity of a thousand-sided polygon, the chiliagon, and distinguish it from one of nine hundred and ninety-nine, although we cannot imagine either of the two (<em>Meditations on First Philosophy</em>, VI). The concept reaches where the image does not.</p>\n<p><strong>From cases to the thesis, and back again.</strong> Thinking abstractly has two directions. The first goes up from cases to a general thesis: it is what Aristotle called <em>epagōgḗ</em>, which we translate as induction, and he illustrates it thus: if the pilot who knows is the best pilot, and the charioteer who knows is the best charioteer, in general the one who knows is the best in each thing (<em>Topics</em> I, 12, 105a13–16). The second goes down from the thesis to the cases to put it to the test. Look at it in a group project: one classmate did almost everything, another turned up on the last day and all four received an 8. It seems unfair to you, just like the match in which the coach gave the same to those who train as to those who do not. From those cases you go up to a thesis: ‘it is just to give to each according to what they contribute’. Now go back down with it to other cases. A classmate could not contribute because he was in hospital for two weeks: does he deserve a lower mark? When you share out a pizza in the youth club, does each person eat according to what they paid, or in equal parts? If the thesis yields results that you yourself reject, it must be refined: perhaps ‘according to what each contributes, except when someone does not contribute for reasons beyond their control’, or perhaps the justice of the classroom is not the justice of sharing out a pizza. That back-and-forth is philosophical work: the case puts the thesis to the test, and the thesis orders the cases. Aristotle already distinguished justice that distributes according to merit from justice that equalises (<em>Nicomachean Ethics</em> V, 3–4).</p>\n<p>Myth thinks above all with <strong>concrete figures</strong>: lightning is the weapon of an angry god, and in Basque mythology storms are unleashed by Mari from her cave or brought by Odei. The first philosophers, on the other hand, began to talk about things that cannot be pointed at. Thales still chooses something visible, water; but his disciple <a href=\"#biografias/anaximandro\">Anaximander</a> proposes as the principle the <em>apeiron</em>, ‘the indeterminate’, something that is neither water nor fire nor anything we see. It is a pure concept, and with it the question stops being ‘who made it?’ and becomes ‘what do all things have in common?’. That is the form of philosophical questions: <em>what is justice?</em> is not answered by pointing to a particular trial, just as <em>what is a dog?</em> is not answered by pointing to Lur. That is why Socrates, when people gave him examples, always asked for what they all share.</p>\n<p><strong>Where did that habit come from?</strong> There are several hypotheses. The <strong>alphabet</strong>, with some twenty letters, put writing within reach of many people. <strong>Money</strong> makes us measure with a single unit things as different as oil, wool or a day’s work; the classicist Richard Seaford has argued, in a disputed thesis, that this habit of seeing ‘the same’ beneath what is different prepared the way for the question of the <em>arkhé</em>. And Egyptian <strong>geometry</strong>, which was used to measure fields, became in Greece a knowledge of triangles that belong to no field. You will study all this, together with the polis and the public square, in Year 2, in <a href=\"#teoria/hf-mito\">‘The emergence of philosophy: from myth to logos’</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Equal to speak, but not everyone.</strong> In democratic Athens of the 5th century BC, two words summed up what it meant to be a citizen: <strong>isonomia</strong>, equality before the law, and <strong>isegoria</strong>, the same right to speak in the assembly. That custom of arguing in public and giving reasons before equals helped philosophy to be born. But 'all' were few: women, slaves and resident foreigners (the metics) were left out. This is shown by <strong><a href=\"#biografias/aspasia\">Aspasia of Miletus</a></strong> (5th century BC), a foreigner and companion of Pericles, to whom ancient sources, from Plato to Plutarch, attribute fame as a teacher of rhetoric and of conversation with Socrates; and yet almost everything we know about her reaches us through the words of others. In Year 2 you will return to Greek citizenship and to Aspasia in the topic on <a href=\"#teoria/hf-sofistas\">the Greek Enlightenment</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>A frontier land.</strong> We must add where it happened. Miletus, Samos and Ephesus were not in mainland Greece, but in <strong>Ionia</strong>, the coast of present-day Turkey, between the kingdom of Lydia, Egypt and the Phoenician routes. From the 8th century BC many Greek cities, with more mouths than land to feed them, sent groups to found <strong>colonies</strong> all over the Mediterranean and the Black Sea; Pliny the Elder calls Miletus ‘the mother of more than ninety cities’. A colony did not simply copy the customs of its mother city: land had to be shared out, laws written and dealings negotiated with peoples of other languages and other gods. Anyone who trades with Egyptians, Lydians and Scythians soon discovers that each people tells the origin of the world in its own way, and it becomes hard to go on believing that one’s own story is the only one possible. What is more, the wealth from trade gave power to families that did not belong to the old aristocracy, and Herodotus tells us that Miletus spent two generations in civil strife: what tradition had once decided now had to be argued over.</p>\n<p><strong>Measuring the sky in order to live on the earth.</strong> A people of farmers and sailors needs to know when to sow and which way to sail. Hesiod, around 700 BC, advised starting the harvest when the Pleiades rise and ploughing when they set (<em>Works and Days</em>, 383-384); Thales is credited with having taught people to find their bearings by the Little Bear and with having predicted an eclipse (that of 585 BC, according to Herodotus). Each city also had its own <strong>calendar</strong>, with lunar months that do not fit the solar year and had to be adjusted as time went on. Anyone who observes the sky for years sees that it repeats itself with a <strong>regularity</strong> that no divine whim explains, and from this arises the idea of a <strong>cosmos</strong>: in Greek, <em>kósmos</em> meant ‘order’ and also ‘adornment’ (hence ‘cosmetics’). Anaximander went so far as to say that things pay one another the penalty for their injustices ‘according to the ordering of time’: he describes nature in the language of a law court, as if the universe had laws just like Solon’s city. Yet none of this is enough on its own: the Egyptians and Babylonians measured the sky better than the Greeks. What was new was asking <em>why</em> the world has order and demanding that the answer be open to discussion, a way of responding to the old human need to make sense of what happens.</p>\n<p><strong>A note on the expression.</strong> The formula ‘from myth to logos’ was popularised by Wilhelm Nestle’s book <em>Vom Mythos zum Logos</em> (1940). Today historians warn that it was not a linear step (see, for example, the volume <em>From Myth to Reason?</em>, edited by Richard Buxton in 1999): myth remained alive in tragedy, in civic religion and in Plato himself, who tells myths in his dialogues.</p>\n<p><strong>Why in Greece?</strong> The Greeks learnt geometry and astronomy from Egypt, but philosophy was born in Miletus and not on the banks of the Nile. The reading in dialogue form <a href=\"#lecturas/fil-lec-tales-escriba\">‘Thales and the Scribe’</a> imagines the farewell between Thales and his Egyptian teacher and sets two worlds face to face: myths that no one questions and a pharaoh whom no one holds to account, against the question, the public square and dialogue between equals. To go over the difference between myth and logos step by step, you have the hint <a href=\"#pistas/fil-mito-logos\">‘What changes in the move from myth to logos?’</a>.</p>\n<h3>Was philosophy born only in Greece?</h3>\n<p>The answer depends on what we mean by ‘philosophy’, and it is best to decide that <strong>before</strong> looking at the cases, so as not to cheat. There are two possible criteria. The criterion of <strong>genealogy</strong> asks where a tradition comes from: philosophy would be whatever descends from Thales, Plato and Aristotle. By that criterion the answer is ‘only in Greece’ by definition, and that proves nothing. The criterion of <strong>method</strong> asks how the work is done: philosophy is any enquiry that answers general questions with <strong>arguments</strong> that have an author, are open to criticism and can be rebutted by others. This is the criterion this topic uses (section 1), and applied honestly it lets in other traditions from the same period, the one <a href=\"#biografias/jaspers\">Karl Jaspers</a> called the <strong>Axial Age</strong> (8th-2nd centuries BC).</p>\n<ul> <li><strong>China: Mohism.</strong> <strong>Mozi</strong> (c. 470-391 BC) argued with the followers of <a href=\"#biografias/confucio\">Confucius</a> without appealing to tradition: he proposed three tests for judging any doctrine (whether it agrees with what the sages of the past did, whether the eyes and ears of ordinary people confirm it, and whether applying it benefits the people) and with them he attacked, for example, the extremely expensive funerals that were ruining families. His followers, the <strong>later Mohists</strong>, studied how to argue well: what a definition is, when an analogy holds, why a piece of reasoning fails. On the other great Chinese master you have the exploration <a href=\"#teoria/hf-confucio\">‘Confucius: opening the canon beyond the West’</a>.</li> <li><strong>India: regulated debate (nyāya).</strong> The <strong>nyāya</strong> school (‘rule’, ‘method’), whose <em>Nyāya Sūtra</em> are attributed to Akṣapāda Gautama (between the 2nd century BC and the 2nd century AD), asked what the reliable sources of knowledge are (perception, inference, comparison and testimony) and set out the form of an argument in five steps. The classic example: ‘there is fire on the hill; because smoke comes from it; where there is smoke there is fire, as in a kitchen; there is smoke of that kind on the hill; therefore there is fire on the hill’. It also distinguished <strong>honest debate</strong>, which seeks the truth, from debate that only wants to win and debate that only wants to destroy the opposing thesis. In India, Buddhists and materialists (<strong>cārvāka</strong>), who denied any life after death, also took part in the debate.</li> </ul>\n<p><strong>The double standard.</strong> It is sometimes said that those traditions ‘do not count’ because they reach us incomplete or through those who opposed them. But exactly the same is true of the Presocratics: not a single line of Thales survives, and almost everything we know about the first Greek philosophers was passed on by much later authors, starting with Aristotle, who quoted them in order to correct them. Demanding of others what you do not demand of your own side is applying a double standard. A criterion, if it is a good one, holds for everyone: including for leaving out, for example, the collections of advice by Egyptian scribes, which teach how to live prudently but do not argue or allow any reply.</p>\n<p><strong>The African debate.</strong> In 1945 the Belgian missionary <strong>Placide Tempels</strong> published <em>Bantu Philosophy</em>, in which he attributed to the Bantu peoples a shared world view centred on ‘vital force’. He wanted to give value to those cultures, and he started a current that came to be called <strong>ethnophilosophy</strong>: reconstructing the implicit philosophy of a people from its proverbs, rites and languages. In 1976 the Beninese philosopher <strong>Paulin Hountondji</strong> criticised it harshly in <em>Sur la ‘philosophie africaine’</em>: a philosophy that belongs to a whole people, that nobody has formulated and with which everyone would agree is not philosophy, but a construction of the researcher who claims to discover it. African philosophy exists, he argued, but it is the philosophy written and debated by Africans with names and surnames. Others objected that in this way he was once again measuring Africa by a European yardstick; the Kenyan <strong>Henry Odera Oruka</strong> sought a middle way by interviewing particular village sages who reasoned for themselves, sometimes against the opinion of their community. The debate remains open.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: could you do an ‘ethnophilosophy’ of Euskal Herria based on Basque sayings and mythology? Would that be Basque philosophy, or would we have to look for particular people who have argued for and debated their ideas?</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t1-117\"><strong>Miracle or process?</strong> The thesis that philosophy is solely Greek is an old one. Diogenes Laertius, around the 3rd century, opens his <em>Lives of the Eminent Philosophers</em> with those who had it born among the ‘barbarians’ (the Persian magi, the Chaldeans, the gymnosophists of India, the druids), and replies that these achievements are Greek, because it was with the Greeks that ‘not only philosophy but the human race itself’ began (I, 3). Hegel, in his <em>Lectures on the History of Philosophy</em>, left Eastern thought in the waiting room, because he saw it as bound up with religion, and in 1883 Ernest Renan spoke of the <strong>‘Greek miracle’</strong> (<em>Recollections of My Youth</em>). Criticism has come from two directions. Hellenists such as M. L. West (1971) and Walter Burkert (1984) have traced what the first Greeks took from Mesopotamia, Persia and Egypt, and a miracle with so many borrowings looks more like a process. And the historian Peter K. J. Park (2013) has shown that textbooks such as Johann Jakob Brucker’s (1742) still began with the Egyptians, Chaldeans and Indians: the ‘Greece only’ boundary was drawn between 1780 and 1830. Those who defend Greek uniqueness reply that what was new was not the ideas but the habit of discussing them in public. The debate remains open, and what it asks of us is to compare texts, not to repeat labels.</p>\n<p><strong>China: knowing what you do not know.</strong> The <em>Analects</em> of Confucius (551-479 BC) are not just advice on good conduct. To his disciple Zilu he teaches what knowing is: to hold as known what you know and to admit that you do not know what you do not know (2.17). It is a thesis about knowledge, very close to what Socrates says in Plato’s <em>Apology of Socrates</em>: that he, at least, does not think he knows what he does not know (21d). The <em>Daodejing</em>, which tradition attributes to <a href=\"#biografias/lao_zi\">Laozi</a> and which took shape over centuries (bamboo slips found in a tomb at Guodian, from around 300 BC, already contain part of it), opens with a warning about language: ‘the <em>dao</em> that can be spoken is not the constant <em>dao</em>’ (ch. 1). The <em>dao</em>, ‘the way’, is the order of everything that exists, and no word can encompass it. Chapter 2 adds an argument: when everyone recognises the beautiful as beautiful, the ugly appears; difficult and easy, long and short, are defined by each other. Think of the word ‘tall’: at primary school you were tall, and in a basketball team you are short without having changed height. If our concepts come in pairs and depend on a comparison, whoever clings to just one sees only half of things.</p>\n<p><strong>The butterfly dream.</strong> Zhuangzi (4th century BC) wrote in stories, but his stories are arguments. At the end of chapter 2 of the book that bears his name, he tells how he once dreamt he was a butterfly, happily fluttering about, without knowing he was Zhuang Zhou; on waking, he was Zhuang Zhou again, and no longer knew whether he was Zhou who had dreamt of being a butterfly or a butterfly dreaming of being Zhou. It is the doubt that Descartes will use in the first of his <em>Meditations on First Philosophy</em> (1641): there is no sure sign that separates waking from dreaming. The ways out differ: Descartes looks for a certainty that can withstand doubt; Zhuangzi accepts that there is a difference between him and the butterfly and calls it ‘the transformation of things’. In chapter 17 he argues with his friend Huizi, a logician famous for his paradoxes. Beside the river Hao, Zhuangzi says the fish are swimming happily. Huizi objects: ‘You are not a fish: how do you know they are happy?’ Zhuangzi replies: ‘You are not me: how do you know I don’t know?’ This is the <strong>problem of other minds</strong>, the same one that arises when you say your dog is pleased or guess from a message that someone is ‘fine’: what allows us to know what someone else feels?</p>\n<p><strong>India: salt in water.</strong> The <em>Upanishads</em> are texts in Sanskrit that close the <em>Vedas</em>; the oldest, the <em>Bṛhadāraṇyaka</em> and the <em>Chāndogya</em>, are usually dated to around the 7th-6th centuries BC. They ask what is truly real, and answer by joining two terms: <em>brahman</em>, the ground of everything that exists, and <em>ātman</em>, the self, what each of us is at the deepest level. In the <em>Chāndogya</em> (6.13) the teacher Uddālaka asks his son Śvetaketu to put salt in water and bring it to him the next day. The salt can no longer be seen, but the water tastes salty at the top, in the middle and at the bottom. That is how the subtle is in all things, says the father, even if you do not see it; and he concludes: <em>tat tvam asi</em>, ‘that is what you are’. It is not a proof, but it is an analogy that can be examined and argued over. In the <em>Bṛhadāraṇyaka</em> the sage Yājñavalkya asks his wife Maitreyī: ‘by what could one know the knower?’ (2.4.14). If everything I know is an object, the knower cannot be one more of those objects, just as the eye does not see itself. And in book 3 the same Yājñavalkya submits to a public debate at the court of King Janaka, where a woman, Gārgī Vācaknavī, asks him the hardest questions.</p>\n<p><strong>Nāgasena’s chariot.</strong> The <a href=\"#biografias/buda\">Buddha</a> (6th-5th centuries BC; the dates are disputed) denied precisely what the <em>Upanishads</em> were looking for: there is no fixed <em>ātman</em> beneath the changes. This is the doctrine of non-self (<em>anātman</em>; in Pali, <em>anattā</em>). Its best-known defence is in the <em>Questions of Milinda</em>, a dialogue in Pali between the monk Nāgasena and Menander, a Greek king who ruled in north-west India in the 2nd century BC. The king has arrived by chariot, and Nāgasena asks him whether the chariot is the pole, the wheels, the axle, all of them together or something apart. To each, the king answers no. So ‘chariot’ is a name for a set of parts, and ‘Nāgasena’ a name for a set of body, sensations and thoughts. Hume will reach something similar in 1739: the self is a ‘bundle’ of perceptions (<em>A Treatise of Human Nature</em>, I, 4, 6). Try it with your group of friends: if over the years all its members change, is it still the same group? Nāgārjuna (around the 2nd century AD) took the idea further: nothing exists by itself, everything depends on something else, and that is emptiness (<em>śūnyatā</em>). His <em>Fundamental Verses on the Middle Way</em> begin by examining the four ways in which something could arise (from itself, from another, from both or without a cause) and rejecting them all (1.1). This is logic, and of the most demanding kind.</p>\n<p><strong>The materialists who doubted the smoke.</strong> The <em>cārvāka</em>, also called <em>lokāyata</em> (‘those who keep to this world’), held that only perception gives sure knowledge, that consciousness arises from the body as the power to intoxicate arises from mixing certain ferments, and that with death everything ends. Their sharpest argument attacks the favourite example of the <em>nyāya</em> school, the one about smoke and fire. To infer fire from smoke you need to know that <strong>always</strong>, where there is smoke there is fire; they called this universal connection <em>vyāpti</em>. How is it known? Not by perception, because no one has seen all the smoke there has been and will be; nor by another inference, because that would need yet another universal connection, and so on without end. It is the problem Hume will raise in 1748 (<em>An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding</em>, IV): that the sun has risen every day does not prove it will rise tomorrow. One important detail: their works have been lost, and we know them mainly through their opponents, such as the summary that opens the <em>Sarvadarśanasaṃgraha</em> (‘Compendium of All Doctrines’), attributed to Mādhava (14th century). The same thing happens with the Presocratics, and what was said about double standards applies here too.</p>\n<p><strong>A philosopher with a name: Zera Yacob.</strong> Hountondji’s criterion, which demanded named authors and written arguments, is answered by an Ethiopian text from the 17th century. Zera Yacob (1599-1692), born near Aksum and persecuted in the religious struggles of the reign of Susenyos, spent two years hiding in a cave and, by his own account, did his own thinking there. His <em>Hatata</em> (‘inquiry’, 1667), written in Ge’ez, reasons like this: each religion says that its own is the true one and that the others lie; they cannot all be right; so it is not enough to believe what we were taught, we must examine it with reason, which the Creator gave to everyone equally. With this criterion he criticises the rules that seem to him contrary to nature, such as the one that held women to be impure during menstruation or the one that placed celibacy above marriage. The text also has its own debate: in 1920 the orientalist Carlo Conti Rossini attributed it to an Italian missionary, Giusto d’Urbino; the Jesuit Claude Sumner defended it as authentic in the 1970s, and in this century the historian Anaïs Wion has returned to the forgery thesis. The question is not settled, and it leaves a double lesson: look for the texts before saying they do not exist, and check who wrote them before celebrating them.</p>\n<p><strong>Ubuntu: I am because we are.</strong> In the Nguni languages of southern Africa, such as Zulu and Xhosa, there is a saying: <em>umuntu ngumuntu ngabantu</em>, ‘a person is a person through other people’. From it comes <em>ubuntu</em>, usually translated as ‘humanity’. The word entered politics when South Africa’s Interim Constitution (1993), as the country left <em>apartheid</em> behind, spoke of the need for ‘<em>ubuntu</em> but not for victimisation’, and Desmond Tutu used it to explain the Truth and Reconciliation Commission (<em>No Future Without Forgiveness</em>, 1999). Is this philosophy, or the ethnophilosophy that Hountondji criticised? African philosophers argue about it. The Nigerian Ifeanyi Menkiti (1984) held that one is not born a person but becomes one by fulfilling one’s duties to the community; the Ghanaian Kwame Gyekye (1992) replied that this leaves the individual defenceless against the group and proposed a moderate communitarianism, with rights the group cannot take away. And Thaddeus Metz, who teaches in South Africa, formulated <em>ubuntu</em> in 2007 as a principle that can be tested against cases: an action is right if it respects and promotes communal life, and wrong if it breaks it. Try it with your class group: would you be the same without them? And what happens when the group gets it wrong?</p>\n<p><strong>Something to think about:</strong> Diogenes Laertius, Hegel and Renan say that philosophy is Greek; West, Burkert and Park, that the boundary is blurrier. What fact would make you change your mind, one way or the other? Choose one of the arguments in this section (Uddālaka’s salt, Nāgasena’s chariot, Zhuangzi’s fish, the <em>cārvāka</em> doubt about smoke or Zera Yacob’s reason) and reconstruct it as premises and a conclusion. Is it an argument, an analogy or a story? Does it stop being philosophy because it is a story, when the allegory of the cave does not? And if a tradition strikes you as ‘wisdom, but not philosophy’, do you ask of it the same as you ask of Thales, of whom not a single line survives?</p>\n<h3>Dictionary</h3>\n<table class=\"glos\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Abstraction</td><td>Operation of the mind that keeps what is common to several things and sets aside what distinguishes them.</td></tr><tr><td>Concept</td><td>General mental representation that results from abstracting; it holds for all cases of a type (dog, justice, number).</td></tr><tr><td>Axial Age</td><td>Name Karl Jaspers gave to the 8th-2nd centuries BC, when forms of reflective thought arose at the same time in Greece, China, India and the Near East.</td></tr><tr><td>Mohism</td><td>Chinese school founded by Mozi (5th century BC): it judges doctrines by tests and studies the rules of argument.</td></tr><tr><td>Nyāya</td><td>Indian school that studies the reliable sources of knowledge and sets out the rules of argument and debate.</td></tr><tr><td>Ethnophilosophy</td><td>Attempt to reconstruct the collective, implicit philosophy of a people from its language, rites and proverbs; criticised by Hountondji (1976).</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The step from myth to logos was not only the business of those who looked at the sky: there was also a “myth to logos” in <strong>politics</strong>. In sixth-century-BC Athens, <strong><a href=\"#biografias/solon\">Solon</a></strong> (c. 638-558 BC) was counted among the <strong>Seven Sages</strong>. Elected <strong>archon</strong> with full powers (594 BC), he replaced the <strong>private vengeance</strong> between clans and Draco’s very harsh penalties with <strong>written, public laws</strong>, equal for all and open to debate in the assembly; he cancelled the debts that enslaved the poor (the <em>seisachtheia</em>) and distributed offices by wealth, not by birth. Just as Thales looks for the natural cause instead of the god, Solon puts the <strong>common law</strong> where the force of the strongest used to rule.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: why is it progress for rules to be <strong>written</strong> and in plain view of everyone, rather than kept by a few? Is it enough for a law to be legal for it to be just? You will meet Solon again in Year 2, when you study the birth of democracy and the Greek Enlightenment.</p>\n<h2>3. Types of knowledge</h2>\n<p>There is no single way of knowing. <strong>Three types of knowledge</strong> are usually distinguished, which are within reach of anyone and which do not exclude one another but complement each other.</p>\n<ul> <li><strong>Common knowledge or common sense.</strong> It is the spontaneous knowledge of everyday life: we know that fire burns and that water makes things wet without needing to study it. We all have it and it is very useful, but it is <strong>unreflective</strong> and tends towards prejudice and conformism. It should not be taken as complete or definitive knowledge, although it is a legitimate <strong>starting point</strong>: currents such as pragmatism or phenomenology start precisely from everyday experience.</li> <li><strong>Scientific knowledge.</strong> It investigates the <strong>proximate causes</strong> of facts, within a field that can be observed and experimented on. It is deeper and more rigorous than common sense (for example, it tells us that water is a molecule made of two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom). It is <strong>partial</strong> knowledge: each science studies only one portion of reality (medicine, the health of the body; psychology, the mind and behaviour; ecology, the relationship between living beings and their environment).</li> <li><strong>Philosophical knowledge.</strong> It studies the <strong>first principles and ultimate causes</strong> of reality. Unlike the sciences, which divide knowledge into portions, philosophy seeks a <strong>worldview</strong>, that is, an understanding of reality <strong>as a whole</strong>, going beyond the apparent to understand the depths of things.</li> </ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr> <th></th> <th><strong>Common knowledge</strong></th> <th><strong>Scientific knowledge</strong></th> <th><strong>Philosophical knowledge</strong></th> </tr> </thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr> <td>What does it seek?</td> <td>To get by in daily life</td> <td>The proximate causes of facts</td> <td>The first principles and ultimate causes</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>How does it proceed?</td> <td>Spontaneously, without reflection</td> <td>Through observation and experiment</td> <td>Through reasoning and argumentation</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Scope</td> <td>The evident and everyday</td> <td>A portion of reality (partial)</td> <td>Reality as a whole (global)</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Is it critical?</td> <td>Little: it tends towards prejudice and conformism</td> <td>Yes, within its field</td> <td>Yes, and it also criticises itself</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Example</td> <td>“Fire burns, water makes things wet”</td> <td>“Water is H₂O”</td> <td>“What is matter? What is truth?”</td> </tr> </tbody> </table>\n<h2>4. The characteristics of philosophical knowledge</h2>\n<p>Philosophy has a series of features that define it and distinguish it from other kinds of knowledge:</p>\n<ul> <li><strong>Rational.</strong> It relies on <strong>reason</strong> and on <strong>arguments</strong>, not on faith, emotion or authority. Since all human beings can reason, in principle we can all philosophise.</li> <li><strong>Critical.</strong> It examines and questions beliefs and truths accepted “just because”, including one’s own. It rejects dogmatism and distrusts what society accepts automatically.</li> <li><strong>Radical.</strong> It goes <strong>to the root</strong> of problems (in Latin, <em>radix</em> means “root”). It does not settle for the superficial: it seeks the foundation of difficult questions such as freedom, good and evil or the meaning of existence.</li> <li><strong>Universal.</strong> It is not limited to one subject or one portion: it is interested in <strong>all</strong> of reality and addresses <strong>everyone</strong>.</li> <li><strong>Systematic.</strong> It tries to <strong>order</strong> and relate to one another the different fields of reality and of human experience, seeking a coherent, overall vision.</li> <li><strong>Practical.</strong> It is not only theory: it also reflects on <strong>how we should live</strong>. Applied to individual conduct, it gives rise to <strong>ethics</strong>; applied to life in common, to <strong>political philosophy</strong>.</li> </ul>\n<p>To this we may add that philosophy is an <strong>open</strong> kind of knowledge (many of its proposals are not closed, definitive truths, but remain subject to debate) and a <strong>historical</strong> one (it constantly revises itself and is never considered finished). This is not <strong>relativism</strong>: the fact that answers are revised does not mean that they are all equally good, because some are better argued than others.</p>\n<p><strong>Do philosophical questions have answers?</strong> The fact that a question is still being debated does not mean that it makes no difference what answer is given. Compare mathematics: Goldbach’s conjecture (every even number greater than 2 is the sum of two primes) has remained unproved since 1742, and nobody thinks that this makes it a matter of taste: it is either true or false, even though we do not know today which. Many philosophers believe that something similar happens with their questions: either we are free in some sense or we are not, and the correct answer does not depend on what each person prefers. Not everyone accepts this: the philosophers of the <strong>Vienna Circle</strong>, in the 1930s, held that many metaphysical questions are meaningless because no experience could settle them, and Wittgenstein thought that a good many philosophical problems are not solved but <em>dissolved</em> when language is clarified (you will see this in Year 2, in <a href=\"#teoria/hf-analitica\">analytic philosophy</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>Does philosophy make progress?</strong> It is the age-old objection: after twenty-five centuries, we are still stuck with the same questions. There are at least three answers. First: it progresses by <strong>ruling things out</strong>; today we know that certain answers do not work, such as the definition of knowledge that Gettier put in trouble in 1963 (<a href=\"#teoria/fil-t3\">Topic 3</a>) or the idea that any property forms a set, which Russell’s paradox sank (<a href=\"#teoria/fil-t4\">Topic 4</a>). Second: it progresses by <strong>refining</strong> questions with distinctions that did not exist before, such as the one that separates a valid argument from one with a true conclusion. Third: when a question finds a method for being solved, it <strong>moves house</strong>; Bertrand Russell observed in 1912 that as soon as a subject allows definite knowledge, it stops being called philosophy and becomes a science, so that philosophy is left, almost by definition, with the questions that are still open. Even so, the philosopher David Chalmers (2015) admits that on the big questions specialists agree much less than in physics or biology, and that this calls for an explanation.</p>\n<h2>5. The branches of philosophy</h2>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t1-32\">Because its object is so broad, philosophy is divided into several <strong>branches</strong> or disciplines, each centred on a type of question:</p>\n<ul> <li><strong>Metaphysics.</strong> It studies reality as such: what “being” means, what exists and what the ultimate properties of things are.</li> <li><strong>Theory of knowledge (epistemology or gnoseology).</strong> It investigates what knowledge is, where it comes from, how far it reaches and what truth is. The three names are not exact synonyms in every context: in the Anglo-Saxon tradition <em>epistemology</em> covers the whole theory of knowledge, whereas in Spanish ‘epistemología’ is sometimes reserved for scientific knowledge.</li> <li><strong>Ethics.</strong> It reflects on good and evil, on the foundation of moral norms and on how we should act.</li> <li><strong>Political philosophy.</strong> It deals with life in community: power, justice, forms of government and the best way of organising society.</li> <li><strong>Aesthetics.</strong> It studies beauty and art, and what our judgements about the beautiful, the ugly or the sublime are based on.</li> <li><strong>Logic.</strong> It analyses the <strong>form</strong> and <strong>correctness</strong> of reasoning, in order to distinguish valid reasoning from invalid.</li> <li><strong>Philosophical anthropology.</strong> It asks what a human being is, considering it from many points of view (biological, social and cultural).</li> </ul>\n<p>There are, besides, other more specialised branches, such as the philosophy of language, the philosophy of science, the philosophy of law, the philosophy of religion or the philosophy of history.</p>\n<p>The branches were not born at the same time. The oldest is usually considered to be <strong>metaphysics</strong>: the pre-Socratics already asked about the <em>arkhé</em>, and Aristotle called it <strong>first philosophy</strong> (the name ‘metaphysics’ is due to the editors of his works). And the youngest is held to be <strong>philosophical anthropology</strong>: Kant summed up philosophy in four questions that culminated in <em>what is the human being?</em>, but only in the 20th century, with <strong>Max Scheler</strong> (<em>Man’s Place in Nature</em>, 1928), did it become established as a discipline of its own.</p>\n<h2>6. Philosophy and the other kinds of knowledge</h2>\n<p>Philosophy does not compete with other kinds of knowledge: it dialogues with them. It shares with <strong>science</strong> the use of reason and the search for explanations, but differs in that science studies specific, verifiable portions of reality, whereas philosophy deals with more general questions and foundations that experiment alone cannot resolve (for example, <em>what is truth</em> or <em>what is right</em>). Some authors even maintain that philosophy and science were, and should again be, “two sides of the same coin”; this is a debatable thesis, not an established fact.</p>\n<p>That unity has a historical explanation. At first philosophy dealt with <strong>everything</strong>, and the sciences gradually became <strong>independent</strong> of it as they found a method of their own: physics, which for centuries was called <strong>natural philosophy</strong> and found its method with the <strong>Scientific Revolution</strong> of the 17th century (Newton’s great work is still titled <em>Mathematical Principles of Natural Philosophy</em>, 1687), and, in the 19th century, psychology or sociology. What is left to philosophy are the questions that none of them can settle on its own.</p>\n<p><strong>Describing is not prescribing.</strong> Science says how things <strong>are</strong>; it does not say how they <strong>ought</strong> to be. A study can calculate how much CO₂ the plane on the end-of-year trip emits or how many hours of sleep a teenager loses by sleeping with their phone next to them, but from those data alone it does not follow what we <em>ought</em> to do: we also need a premise about what is worthwhile (health, the planet, each person’s freedom). David Hume already warned in the 18th century that many arguments pass without warning from ‘is’ to ‘ought’, and that this step needs justification. That is why science cannot set its own <strong>priorities</strong> on its own either: deciding whether to research a vaccine or a cosmetic first, or what limits to place on experiments with embryos, is an ethical and political decision (<a href=\"#teoria/fil-t3\">Topic 3</a>, ‘Science, technology, art and society’).</p>\n<p><strong>An influence in both directions.</strong> Philosophy helps science: <strong>epistemology</strong> asks it what counts as evidence (think of the <a href=\"#teoria/fil-t3-semmelweis\">Semmelweis case</a>), and some philosophical ideas have even changed physics; Einstein acknowledged that reading Hume helped him to distrust absolute time before he formulated relativity. And science changes philosophy: after Darwin it is impossible to think in the same way about what a human being is, and neuroscience experiments on decision-making have reopened the debate about whether we are free (<a href=\"#teoria/fil-metafisica\">Topic M</a>, section 7). The lesson is twofold: a philosophy that ignores the science of its time is talking into a void, and a science that does not question its concepts and its aims is advancing blindly.</p>\n<p><strong>The borderline case of mathematics.</strong> Mathematics resembles philosophy in that it is not done in a laboratory, and science in that its results, once proved, are no longer disputed. The difference lies in the starting point: someone doing mathematics starts from <strong>axioms</strong> accepted by everyone working in that system, whereas the philosopher argues precisely about the <strong>premises</strong>. Euclid’s <em>Elements</em> (c. 300 BC), which deduce the whole of geometry from a few definitions and postulates, were for centuries the model of rigour that Spinoza imitated in his <em>Ethics</em> (<a href=\"#teoria/fil-spinoza-sistema\">the geometrical order</a>). But in the 19th century Lobachevsky, Bolyai and Riemann built consistent geometries by denying the fifth postulate, the parallel postulate: the axioms turned out to be chosen starting points, not self-evident truths. This is where the <strong>philosophy of mathematics</strong> comes in, asking questions that no proof can close: do numbers exist outside our minds or do we invent them? why do formulae written on a whiteboard describe the motion of the planets so well? The physicist Eugene Wigner (1960) called this last point the ‘unreasonable effectiveness’ of mathematics.</p>\n<p><strong>Two kinds of questions about the same thing.</strong> ‘How many neurons does the human brain have?’ has an answer obtained by counting: some 86 billion, according to the count published in 2009 by the team of the neuroscientist Suzana Herculano-Houzel, which dissolved brain tissue and counted the nuclei of the cells. ‘What is it to have a mind?’ is not answered by counting anything. We could know each of those neurons and still not know whether a mind is the brain functioning, something the brain produces, or something a machine could also have. The first is an <strong>empirical</strong> question: it is settled by observing and measuring. The second is <strong>conceptual</strong>: it asks what we mean by a word and what conditions something must meet to fall under it. Many conceptual questions are also <strong>second-order</strong>: they are not about the world, but about how a science studies it. ‘How many species of beetle are there?’ is first-order; ‘what is a species?’ or ‘what counts as explaining something?’ are second-order. Neither kind is more important: conceptual questions without data are left hanging in the air, and data without clear concepts do not tell us what they measure. A count of ‘happy people’ depends on what was understood by happiness before the survey was carried out.</p>\n<p><strong>A philosophy for each science.</strong> Each science works with concepts that it uses without fully defining them, and that is where its philosophy begins. The <strong>philosophy of biology</strong> asks, for example, what a species is. Ernst Mayr proposed in 1942 that it is a group of populations that can interbreed and are isolated from others; but bacteria reproduce without interbreeding, and for them we must seek another criterion. It also asks what a function is: we say that the heart serves to pump blood, not to make noise, although it does both. What does that ‘serves to’ mean in a nature without intentions? The <strong>philosophy of physics</strong> asks what time is. Newton thought of it as something that flows uniformly, whether or not there are things; Leibniz replied, in his correspondence with Samuel Clarke (1715–1716), that time is only the order in which things succeed one another, and that without things there would be no time. Einstein’s relativity has changed the terms of the debate, but has not closed it. The <strong>philosophy of mind</strong> asks whether an explanation made of neurons can say what a pain feels like. Thomas Nagel raised it in 1974 with a bat: even if we knew everything about how it orientates itself by echoes, we would not know what it is like to be a bat.</p>\n<p><strong>When one piece of data is not enough: two cases.</strong> In August 2006 the International Astronomical Union, meeting in Prague, voted that Pluto should no longer be a planet. Nothing new had been discovered about Pluto: in 2005 Eris had been found, farther away and with somewhat more mass, and it had to be decided whether both were planets, with many more to come, or neither. The telescopes provided the data; the decision was about a concept. The assembly set three conditions: to orbit the Sun, to have enough mass to be nearly round, and to have cleared the neighbourhood of its orbit of other bodies. Pluto does not meet the third. The second case is more serious. In 1968 a committee of Harvard University proposed that irreversible coma, with the brain showing no activity, should serve as the criterion of death, even if a machine kept the heart beating. Neurology could describe that state; what no instrument could measure was whether that person should be called dead, and on that depended, for example, whether their organs could be donated. Today that criterion is in the law of many countries, Spain among them, and it continues to be debated. In both cases science needed a conceptual decision, and philosophy, in order to make it well, needed to know exactly what the scientists were measuring.</p>\n<p>With <strong>religion</strong> it shares an interest in the great questions (the meaning of life, death, the origin of the world), but it differs in <strong>method</strong>: religion answers from <strong>faith</strong> and <strong>revelation</strong>, whereas philosophy does so from <strong>reason</strong> and <strong>argument</strong>. With <strong>art</strong> it shares the capacity to wonder and to look at the world in a new way; however, art expresses itself through <strong>works</strong> (images, stories, music) that make us feel, whereas philosophy works with <strong>concepts</strong> and tries to justify rationally what it asserts.</p>\n<p><strong>Faith and reason in the same person.</strong> The fact that the method is different does not force anyone to take sides. There have been great philosophers who were believers, such as Augustine of Hippo, Maimonides, Thomas Aquinas or Kierkegaard, and others who were agnostics or atheists, such as Bertrand Russell or Jean-Paul Sartre; in a philosophy lesson nobody has to leave their beliefs at the door, but nor can they use them as an argument. What is more, the God that reason arrives at is usually very different from the God of prayer: Aristotle’s first mover thinks itself and does not listen to prayers. Blaise Pascal felt this so vividly that, in a note from 1654 that he kept sewn into his clothes, he wrote that his God was the God of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob, ‘not of the philosophers and the scholars’. Between the two lies <strong>theology</strong>, which argues rigorously, but from revealed premises that it does not question; Thomas Aquinas called it a science because it starts from principles that someone else knows better, as the musician relies on the calculations of the mathematician (<em>Summa Theologiae</em> I, q. 1, a. 2). And there is another difference: in philosophy, being wrong is not a sin; a false thesis is corrected, not confessed.</p>\n<p><strong>What is a religion?</strong> Religion is often defined as belief in a Supreme Being and in its relationship with human beings. This is too narrow a definition. There are religions with many gods, such as the Greek religion of Homer, and others that rest on the sacred character of the world, of a mountain, a river or an ancestor, rather than on a creator god; Buddhism does not recognise any creator god. For this reason the sociologist Émile Durkheim preferred to define it by the sacred: a system of beliefs and practices concerning things that are ‘separate, forbidden’, which unites those who share them into one community (<em>The Elementary Forms of Religious Life</em>, 1912, book I, ch. 1). That last part is decisive: a religion is not only a body of knowledge, but a <strong>social institution</strong>, which has said who belonged to the community, what was right and what wrong, and why the one who rules had the right to rule. Neither philosophy nor science has ever had such power. With philosophy it shares the ultimate questions; what changes is where the answer comes from and who guarantees it. Religion starts from a <strong>revelation</strong>, received in a sacred text, a rite or a tradition, and it is guarded by an <strong>authority</strong> that fixes certain points as <strong>dogma</strong>, truths that are not discussed, as the Council of Nicaea did in 325 when it fixed the Christian creed. In philosophy no thesis is safe from revision, and an author is worth what their arguments are worth.</p>\n<p><strong>Religion and theology are not the same thing.</strong> Each religion has its <strong>theology</strong>: the reflection that explains how its myths, its rites and its beliefs relate to one another, and what meaning they have. It is not a Christian or university invention: those who have directed the religious life of a people, priests, druids or shamans, have also been its theologians, those who kept and explained to others the mysteries of their faith. That is why within a religion there are two kinds of knowledge. One deals with its object: what the divine is, what the human being is, what the world is like and where it is going. The other deals with its own <strong>coherence</strong>: how some beliefs fit with others, how an obscure text is interpreted, what to do when two teachings seem to clash. Can a good and all-powerful God allow the suffering of a child? That question, the problem of evil, arises from that second kind of knowledge, because it puts to the test the coherence of three beliefs at once. You have already seen that theology argues from premises it does not discuss, and that marks a boundary for it: it can reinterpret a dogma, but it cannot conclude that it is false without ceasing to do theology. The philosopher has no such boundary. Lactantius attributed to Epicurus that same question about evil (<em>On the Wrath of God</em>, 13), and no one expected from an Epicurean an answer fixed in advance.</p>\n<p><strong>Four ways of relating faith and reason.</strong> The fact that most thinkers of the past were believers does not mean that they all understood the relationship between what is believed and what is reasoned in the same way. There are four classical positions. <strong>Faith alone is enough:</strong> revealed truth is sufficient for knowing and for living well, and philosophy is superfluous or a hindrance. Tertullian, around the year 200, put the question thus: 'What has Athens to do with Jerusalem?' (<em>Prescription against Heretics</em>, 7). The phrase 'I believe because it is absurd', which is attributed to him, is not in his works; the closest thing says that something is certain 'because it is impossible' (<em>On the Flesh of Christ</em>, 5). <strong>Reason in the service of faith:</strong> reason helps to understand what is already believed, but it has no value of its own. Anselm of Canterbury summed it up around 1078: 'I do not seek to understand in order to believe, but I believe in order to understand' (<em>Proslogion</em>, 1). <strong>Two truths with no reconciliation:</strong> faith and reason yield truths of different kinds, and their clashes have no solution. In 1277 the bishop of Paris, Étienne Tempier, condemned those who said that something was true according to philosophy and not according to faith, 'as if there were two contrary truths'; he was pointing at masters such as Siger of Brabant, who probably did not defend it in those terms. <strong>Reason alone is enough:</strong> religious truth is not knowledge, and reason alone is sufficient for knowing and for living well; this was held by the Baron d'Holbach in <em>The System of Nature</em> (1770), published under a false name. Between the second and the third lies the harmony of Thomas Aquinas: two distinct paths which, if both speak truth, cannot contradict each other.</p>\n<p><strong>Something to think about:</strong> choose a question that matters to you, for example 'what happens when we die?'. What part of it could a science answer, what part is conceptual and what part have religions answered? Then imagine that someone says to you: 'justice is everyone having the same'. Find a case that confirms it and another that puts it in difficulty, and reformulate the thesis so that it withstands both. Finally, return to the four positions on faith and reason: in which one do Augustine, Pascal or Russell, whom you have read in this topic, fit? And the way of thinking you hear most around you? Can a person move from one position to another over the course of their life without ceasing to think rigorously?</p>\n<p><strong>Making art and thinking about art.</strong> <strong>Aesthetics</strong> does not teach you to paint or compose: that is the <strong>technique</strong> of each art, and it is learnt in a workshop or a conservatoire. The name was coined by Alexander Baumgarten in 1750, from the Greek <em>aísthesis</em>, ‘sensation’, and its questions are different: what makes something art? are some tastes better than others? what do we need art for? Eduardo Chillida had to know how to forge steel to make the <em>Comb of the Wind</em> (1977); asking whether those three pieces of iron on the rocks of Donostia are beautiful, and why almost everyone thinks so, is aesthetics. The same goes for the urinal that Marcel Duchamp signed and sent to an exhibition in 1917: making it required no technique, and that is exactly why it forced people to ask what a work of art is. A philosopher of art does not have to be able to play the piano, just as a philosopher of science does not have to work in a laboratory.</p>\n<p>It is also worth distinguishing <strong>ethics</strong> from <strong>law</strong>. Both deal with <strong>justice</strong> and with what we ought to do, but they differ in their foundation and in the way they oblige. Legal norms rest on <strong>laws</strong> passed by an authority and are imposed from outside, with <strong>sanctions</strong>; ethical norms rest on <strong>reasons</strong> and oblige from within, through the <strong>conviction</strong> of one’s own conscience. That is why not everything legal is just, nor is everything morally required written into a law.</p>\n<p><strong>Philosophy of law.</strong> If not everything legal is just, a question arises that no legal code answers: is a very unjust law still law? This is the subject of the <strong>philosophy of law</strong>, and the discussion has two sides. For <strong>natural law theory</strong>, above the written laws there are principles of justice that judge them; Augustine of Hippo already wrote that a law that was not just did not seem to him to be a law (<em>On Free Will</em> I, 5). For <strong>legal positivism</strong> (Hans Kelsen, H. L. A. Hart), what the law says is one thing and whether it is good is another, and mixing them up confuses both. After Nazism, the jurist Gustav Radbruch argued (1946) that a law of extreme injustice loses its character as law; Hart replied (1958) that it was more honest to say ‘this is law; but it is too iniquitous to be applied or obeyed’. Both wanted the same thing, that nobody should be able to hide behind ‘I was only following the law’; they disagreed about how to say it. It is the question Solon left open in section 2.</p>\n<p>A good way of seeing the difference is to compare how each kind of knowledge would answer the same question, for example <em>what is the human being?</em>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr> <th>Knowledge</th> <th>How it answers</th> <th>By what method</th> </tr> </thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr> <td>Science</td> <td>Describes the human being as an organism: body, brain, evolution</td> <td>Data, observation and experiments (biology, psychology)</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Religion</td> <td>Understands it as a creature with a meaning and a transcendent destiny</td> <td>Revelation and faith</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Art</td> <td>Expresses the experience of being human and makes us feel it</td> <td>The work (image, story, music…)</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Philosophy</td> <td>Asks what we are and what grounds our freedom and dignity</td> <td>Concepts and rational arguments</td> </tr> </tbody> </table>\n<h2>7. Philosophy throughout history</h2>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t1-44\">The history of philosophy is usually divided into four great periods.</p>\n<ul> <li><strong>Ancient philosophy</strong> (6th century BC – 5th century AD). It begins with the pre-Socratics, who sought the first principle (<em>arkhé</em>) of nature. With <strong>Socrates</strong>, interest shifts towards the human being and the search for universal definitions through dialogue. His successors create the two great systems: the <strong>idealism</strong> of <strong>Plato</strong>, who places true reality in the world of Forms, and the <strong>realism</strong> of <strong>Aristotle</strong>, who starts from sensible experience (these two labels are later: historians put them there, not the philosophers themselves). Then come the Hellenistic schools (Epicureanism and Stoicism), centred on how to live and attain happiness.</li> <li><strong>Medieval philosophy</strong> (5th – 15th century). Its great theme is the relationship between <strong>reason and faith</strong>, that is, between philosophy and religion. <strong>Saint Augustine</strong> stands out in the first period (patristics), and <strong>Saint Thomas Aquinas</strong> in scholasticism. In this period the Christian, Islamic and Jewish traditions also engage in dialogue.</li> <li><strong>Modern philosophy</strong> (15th – 18th century). After the Renaissance, the human being and <strong>knowledge</strong> come to the fore. <strong>Rationalism</strong> (Descartes) emerges, which trusts above all in reason; <strong>empiricism</strong> (Locke, Hume), which starts from experience; and the great synthesis of the two that <strong>Kant</strong> achieves with his “criticism”.</li> <li><strong>Contemporary philosophy</strong> (19th – 21st century). It is a very diverse period: <strong>Marxism</strong> (Marx), which aims to transform society; <strong>vitalism</strong> (Nietzsche); <strong>existentialism</strong> (Sartre, Simone de Beauvoir); <strong>analytic philosophy</strong> (Wittgenstein) or the <strong>Frankfurt School</strong> (Habermas), among many other currents. Women philosophers such as Hannah Arendt or María Zambrano also shine in it.</li> </ul>\n<p>It is worth remembering that the appearance of one period does not erase the earlier ones: Greek philosophy is still, in some way, at the base of all later Western thought.</p>\n<p><strong>What has philosophy believed itself to be?</strong> In twenty-five centuries, philosophy has not only changed its answers: it has also changed its idea of itself. Five conceptions stand out, and none has disappeared entirely:</p>\n<ul> <li><strong>Wisdom and a way of life</strong> (Antiquity). For the Socratics, the Stoics and the Epicureans, to philosophise was to learn how to live: a daily training of attention and desires rather than a theory. The historian Pierre Hadot spoke of ‘spiritual exercises’; the notebooks that the emperor Marcus Aurelius wrote for himself are a good example (<a href=\"#teoria/fil-helenismo\">the Hellenistic schools</a>).</li> <li><strong>Handmaid of theology</strong> (Middle Ages). The Latin formula <em>philosophia ancilla theologiae</em> sums up the idea that reason serves to clarify and defend what faith teaches. Thomas Aquinas qualified it: philosophy and theology have different methods, but if both speak the truth they cannot contradict each other (<a href=\"#teoria/hf-fe-razon\">faith and reason</a>, in Year 2).</li> <li><strong>Science of first principles.</strong> Aristotle called the science of first causes wisdom, and Descartes compared the whole of knowledge to a tree: the roots are metaphysics, the trunk physics, and the branches medicine, mechanics and morals. Philosophy would be the foundation on which the other sciences depend.</li> <li><strong>Critique</strong> (since the Enlightenment). Kant wanted reason to put itself on trial in order to know how far it can go; Marx added that it is not enough to interpret the world, it has to be changed; and the Frankfurt School turned philosophy into a critique of society.</li> <li><strong>Analysis of language</strong> (20th century). For Wittgenstein philosophy is not a body of doctrines but an <strong>activity</strong>: clarifying what we say, because many problems arise from language confusing us.</li> </ul>\n<p>All five coexist today: whoever reads Epictetus to cope better with exam nerves, whoever studies the foundations of physics, whoever analyses what ‘freedom’ means in a debate or whoever criticises an advert is doing philosophy in one of those senses. The fact that philosophy argues about what it is itself is not a defect: it is one more philosophical question, and one of the few that no other discipline can answer for it.</p>\n<p><strong>The spectator at the festival.</strong> In the <em>Tusculan Disputations</em> (V, 3, 8-9), Cicero relates that Leon, ruler of Phlius, wanted to know what Pythagoras’s occupation was. He replied with a simile: our existence resembles the great festivals of the games. Some come to compete and to win fame and a crown; others, to do business by trading; and a small group, the freest of all, pursues neither applause nor riches, but seeks to observe carefully what happens and in what manner. Those who devote their lives to investigating the nature of things he called ‘lovers of wisdom’, that is, philosophers. We find here a first notion of philosophy: to contemplate without any desire for gain, which in Greek was called <em>theōria</em>. Plato and Aristotle also explained what the origin of that contemplation is. In the <em>Theaetetus</em> (155d), Socrates states: ‘this experience, that of wondering, is very characteristic of the philosopher, for philosophy has no other beginning than this’. Aristotle (<em>Metaphysics</em> I, 2, 982b) adds a curious nuance: ‘the lover of myths is, in a sense, a philosopher, since myth is composed of things that cause wonder’. Wonder, then, is common to the teller of myths and to the one who inquires into reasons; the difference lies in the use each makes of it.</p>\n<p><strong>A medicine for the soul.</strong> In the Hellenistic period philosophy was understood above all as a cure. Epicurus opens his <em>Letter to Menoeceus</em> (122) thus: let no one, being young, delay in philosophising, nor, being old, tire of doing so, for no one is too young or too old for the health of the soul. And in another fragment, preserved by Porphyry, he compares the philosopher to the physician: just as a medicine that does not expel the diseases of the body is of no use, so a philosophy that does not expel the suffering of the soul is of no use. Cicero said it in Latin in a brief phrase: philosophy is, without doubt, the medicine of the soul (<em>Tusculan Disputations</em> III, 3, 6). The historian Pierre Hadot maintained that in Antiquity choosing a school was choosing a way of life, and that theories were at the service of that choice: Epicurean physics, for example, was studied in order to lose fear of the gods and of death. Think of the night before an exam: for an Epicurean, the philosophical question would not only be what fear is, but how to cease being its slave.</p>\n<p><strong>Handmaid of theology?</strong> You already saw that the formula <em>philosophia ancilla theologiae</em> sums up a medieval conception. It is worth nuancing it. Peter Damian (11th century), in his work <em>On Divine Omnipotence</em>, maintains, in paraphrase, that dialectic should only be used as a handmaid in the service of her mistress, theology, and never to rule over her. The exact Latin formula, by the way, does not appear literally in him: it is a later summary. His star case is a question: can God make what has happened not to have happened? For Damian, limiting God with the rules of logic would be to give him a master. Thomas Aquinas answers differently in the <em>Summa Theologiae</em> (I, q. 25, a. 4): he cannot, because it implies contradiction. And in a. 3 he specifies that it is more accurate to say that this cannot be done than to say that God cannot do it: the contradictory is not a difficult thing, it is a non-thing. Think of a video game: nobody says that the programmer lacks power for not being able to create a square circle; the phrase simply describes nothing. This links with his general idea (I, q. 1, a. 8, ad 2): grace does not suppress nature, but perfects it; reason retains its own field. Two ways of understanding the relationship: reason as a handmaid who obeys, or as a partner with its own rules.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: does what reason can demand of a belief have limits? And what a belief can demand of reason?</p>\n<p><strong>The tree and the four questions.</strong> Descartes, in the prefatory letter to the <em>Principles of Philosophy</em> (1647), compares all of philosophy to a tree: the roots are metaphysics, the trunk is physics, and the branches are the other sciences, which reduce to three principal ones: medicine, mechanics, and morals. And he adds a surprising idea: the fruits are not picked from the roots or the trunk, but from the tips of the branches; therefore the most useful part of philosophy depends on its last parts. Morals, the highest and most perfect, is for him the last degree of wisdom. Kant, by contrast, in the <em>Logic</em> (Jäsche edition, 1800, Introduction III), distinguishes a scholastic concept of philosophy from a worldly concept, the one that interests everyone, and sums it up in four questions: what can I know?, what ought I to do?, what may I hope?, what is the human being? To each corresponds a discipline: metaphysics, morals, religion, and anthropology. And he says that the first three refer to the last. The first three already appear in the <em>Critique of Pure Reason</em> (A 805/B 833). There is a difference: Descartes orders knowledge from bottom to top, from a foundation; Kant orders the questions around a person. Imagine that you must choose what to study: do you first seek a secure foundation or first what you want to do with your life?</p>\n<p><strong>Transform or clarify?</strong> Marx's Thesis 11 on Feuerbach (written in 1845 and published by Engels in 1888) says that philosophers have only interpreted the world in various ways, and that the point is to change it. Engels added a “but” (<em>aber</em>) when publishing it; the sentence is engraved on Marx's tomb in Highgate. Against that idea, Wittgenstein maintains in the <em>Tractatus</em> (4.112) that philosophy is not a doctrine, but an activity of clarification. In the <em>Philosophical Investigations</em> he describes philosophy as a battle against the bewitchment of our understanding by means of language (§ 109), affirms that it leaves everything as it is (§ 124) and that its goal is to show the fly the way out of the fly-bottle (§ 309). Two positions, two reasons. Whoever defends Marx will say: if in the face of injustice you only clarify concepts, your philosophy becomes complicit with what exists; thinking matters if it changes something. Whoever defends Wittgenstein will reply: without clarity, transforming is acting blindly, and many quarrels arise from misunderstood words, like arguing about “freedom” without knowing what is meant. Perhaps they are not incompatible: clarification can be a step prior to action, or action can create new problems to clarify.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: what helps more to resolve a conflict in your group of friends, acting immediately or first clarifying what each one understands by what is being argued about?</p>\n<p><strong>Does philosophy progress?</strong> You already saw three answers to this objection. Let us see how Bertrand Russell posed it in <em>The Problems of Philosophy</em> (1912, ch. 15), for he turns the question around. For him, the value of philosophy lies not in offering secure answers, but in the uncertainty itself: it frees us from the tyranny of custom, because whoever has never philosophised lives shut up in the prejudices of common sense and of his time. Russell concludes that philosophy is studied for the questions rather than for the answers, since the questions enlarge our idea of what is possible. And today the debate remains open. David Chalmers (2015) maintains that there has been real progress, but little convergence: specialists still do not agree on the great questions, and that needs explanation. Eric Dietrich (2011) is more sceptical: he doubts that progress has been made in essentials. Daniel Stoljar (2017), by contrast, is optimistic and defends that philosophy advances, although its progress does not resemble that of physics. Each position has its reason. Whoever doubts progress points out that we keep discussing the same things; whoever defends it replies that bad answers have been discarded and the questions refined, and that measuring philosophy by the standard of science already takes for granted what is being discussed. Imagine a group of friends who never agree on what plan to make, but who each week discard worse ideas and understand better what each one wants: would we say that it is not progressing?</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: if the value of philosophy lay in the questions, would it make sense to demand that it close any of them? What counts as progress: agreeing or understanding better?</p>\n<p><strong>How the succession of philosophies has been read.</strong> That there are so many different philosophies has been interpreted in very different ways. Aristotle, in the <em>Metaphysics</em> (I 10, 993a15), says that first philosophy, in its beginnings, ‘babbles’: the first thinkers spoke like children who do not yet articulate well, getting things half right. It is a reading of progress: the ancients prepare the way, and whoever comes later polishes what they barely knew how to say. The sceptics read the same thing the other way round. For them, the disagreement (<em>diaphōnía</em>) among philosophers shows that there is no reason to trust any of them. It is the first mode of Agrippa, recorded by Sextus Empiricus in the <em>Outlines of Pyrrhonism</em> (I 165): if on a question the wise disagree without remedy, the prudent course is to suspend judgement. Hegel proposes a third reading, which I here only summarise in my own words. In the Introduction to his <em>Lectures on the History of Philosophy</em>, he argues that this history is not a gallery of disconnected opinions, but a development: each philosophy responds to the previous ones, surpasses them and at the same time preserves what is valuable in them. He calls this movement <em>Aufhebung</em>, a German word meaning at once ‘suppress’, ‘preserve’ and ‘elevate’. Three views of one and the same fact: babbling that improves, disagreement that discourages, development that advances. Think of a long discussion among friends about which film is the best: it may end in nothing, it may teach you to argue, or criteria may gradually be refined.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: is disagreement among philosophers proof that none of them knows anything, or that the question is difficult? What difference would there be?</p>\n<p><strong>Historicity and universality.</strong> Every philosophical question arises at a particular moment and in a particular place. That is its <em>historikotasuna</em> (historicity): Plato did not ask about justice in the same world as we do. But many questions return again and again because they spring from the human condition: we are mortal, ignorant and live together with others. That is its <em>unibertsaltasuna</em> (universality), and that is why we call them <em>galdera erradikalak</em>, radical questions, from the root. Take death. Epicurus (4th–3rd century BC) taught that we should not fear it, because while we exist it is not there, and when it arrives we are no longer there. In the Middle Ages, the central question was salvation: what happens to the soul afterwards. Today, palliative care and bioethics ask how to accompany someone who is dying and who should decide. The context and the answers change; the underlying question, how to live knowing that we will die, remains. There are two risks. The first is <em>anakronismoa</em>: judging the past with today’s categories, such as reproaching Epicurus for not knowing modern medicine. The second is <em>erlatibismoa</em>: believing that nothing has value outside its own period, and therefore that we have nothing to learn from the ancients. Gadamer, in <em>Truth and Method</em> (1960), proposes, in paraphrase, the fusion of horizons: when reading an ancient text we neither enter the author’s head nor impose our own, but rather his horizon and ours meet and widen each other.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: which question from an ancient philosopher seems to you still alive in your life? And which one seems to you to make sense only in its own time?</p>\n<p>We have covered many ways of understanding what philosophising is: watching the party of life without seeking profit, healing the soul, serving another kind of knowledge or being its partner, putting down roots and bearing fruit like a tree, transforming the world or clarifying what we say, advancing or going round in circles, being born at a moment and returning again and again. There is no need to choose just one: the point is to see which one helps you understand what you are doing when you think slowly about something that matters to you.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: 1) Think of a difficult decision this school year, such as what to study afterwards or what to do in the face of a conflict within your group of friends. Which conception would help you most: that of the spectator who first watches calmly, that of the doctor who seeks to calm fear, or that of the person who acts to change things? Why?</p><p class=\"aporia\">2) If you had to explain to someone your age what philosophy is for, would you say that it serves to settle questions, to understand them better, or to live in a different way? What reason would you give and what objection would someone who disagreed raise?</p><p class=\"aporia\">3) When you read an ancient question (about death, justice or friendship), how do you know whether it is still alive in your life or only belongs to its own time? What would you do to avoid falling into anachronism or relativism?</p>\n<p><strong>Who decides who is a philosopher.</strong> The previous stages are those of a <strong>canon</strong>, the list of authors one ‘has to study’, and the canon does not only choose works: it also decided who could be a philosopher. Many eighteenth-century histories of philosophy began with the Egyptians, Chaldeans or Indians; between 1780 and 1830 several German historians removed them from the story and established the idea that philosophy begins in Greece, partly with racial prejudices that nobody would defend today (Peter K. J. Park has studied this, 2013). Something similar happened with women: the letters of Elisabeth of Bohemia to Descartes were not published until 1879, and for two centuries only his replies were read. You have this developed in the activity <a href=\"#teoria/fil-t1-filosofia-mujer\">‘Philosophy and the condition of women’</a> and, in Year 2, in <a href=\"#teoria/hf-canon\">the exploration on the canon</a>. Caution applies in both directions: revising the canon is not a matter of inventing merits, but of rereading the sources.</p>\n<p><strong>Did they really say it?</strong> We need to be just as careful with famous sayings. ‘I disapprove of what you say, but I will defend to the death your right to say it’ is not by Voltaire: his biographer Evelyn Beatrice Hall wrote it in 1906 to sum up his attitude. Galileo’s ‘and yet it moves’ first appears in print in 1757, more than a century after his trial. And you have already seen in section 1 that ‘I only know that I know nothing’ simplifies what Socrates says in the <em>Apology</em>. Every day quotations from Einstein, Aristotle or Nietzsche circulate online that none of them ever wrote. The rule is simple: a philosophical quotation is accepted when the <strong>work</strong> and the <strong>passage</strong> can be given; if nobody can say where it is, we say ‘it is attributed to’ or, better still, we do not use it.</p>\n<h2>8. The functions of philosophy and its value today</h2>\n<p>What is philosophy for in a world dominated by science and technology? Today it is more necessary than ever, and it fulfils at least three <strong>functions</strong>:</p>\n<ul> <li><strong>Critical function.</strong> It teaches us to think for ourselves, to analyse the discourses that surround us (political, advertising, scientific, cultural) and to detect their prejudices and false promises. It protects us against manipulation, fanaticism and propaganda. And it starts at home: philosophy also examines its own assumptions (<strong>self-criticism</strong>).</li> <li><strong>Function of orientation.</strong> It thinks not only about the world as it is, but also about the world as it <strong>could be</strong>. It helps us make important decisions, live with greater freedom and take part in building a fairer and more supportive society.</li> <li><strong>Function of seeking meaning.</strong> It answers the human need to understand who we are and what meaning our lives and our actions have.</li> </ul>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t1-50\">Immanuel Kant summed up the spirit of philosophy in a motto that is still valid:</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>“<em>Sapere aude</em> (dare to know): have the courage to use your own reason.” — Immanuel Kant, <em>An Answer to the Question: What Is Enlightenment?</em> (1784)</p> </blockquote>\n<p><strong>Can philosophy be learnt?</strong> Kant himself answered no, or almost. In the <em>Critique of Pure Reason</em> (A 837/B 865) he explains that philosophy is not a finished body of knowledge that can be learnt the way mathematics is learnt, because it does not exist in any completed book: the only thing that can be learnt is <strong>to philosophise</strong>, that is, to use one’s own reason, with its rules, on the attempts that already exist. Someone who knows a philosopher’s system by heart has, according to Kant, only ‘historical’ knowledge: they are like a plaster cast of a living person. Years earlier, announcing his lectures (1765), he had written that the student should not learn thoughts, but learn to think. That does not make the authors useless: reason is trained with them, just as nobody learns to play chess without studying other people’s games. But passing this topic by repeating the four stages of history, without being able to discuss a single question, would be learning philosophy without learning to philosophise.</p>\n<p>If philosophising is an activity and not a list of doctrines, it can start very early. The American philosopher <strong>Gareth B. Matthews</strong> collected in <em>Philosophy and the Young Child</em> (1980) questions from small children that coincide with classic problems: a six-year-old, while scraping out a saucepan, asked his father how we can be sure that everything is not a dream, the same doubt as Descartes’s. Matthews held that children do not do ‘toy’ philosophy, but real philosophy that we adults stop doing. Starting from similar ideas, Matthew Lipman turned the classroom into a community of inquiry: you have this in the activity <a href=\"#teoria/fil-t1-lipman\">‘Matthew Lipman and philosophy for children’</a>.</p>\n<p>The value of philosophy is so widely recognised today that <strong>UNESCO</strong> (the United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organization) declared <strong>World Philosophy Day</strong> in 2008, which is celebrated on the <strong>third Thursday of November</strong>. For UNESCO, philosophy is a “school of freedom and solidarity”: it trains critical citizens, encourages dialogue between cultures and contributes to peace and to the quality of democracy. Its motto, “building the defences of peace in the minds of men and women”, sums up well why this subject remains necessary.</p>\n<p><strong>And what if it is ‘no use at all’?</strong> It is the objection most often raised against this subject, and it deserves a considered answer. The exploration <a href=\"#teoria/fil-utilidad\">‘Is philosophy useful or valuable?’</a> and its dossier in Readings, <a href=\"#lecturas/fil-lec-utilidad-filosofia\">‘Is philosophy useful? The useful, the valuable and information’</a>, distinguish the useful (a means to something else) from the valuable (what is worth something in itself), and information from knowledge; the hint <a href=\"#pistas/fil-utilidad\">‘What is philosophy for if it produces nothing?’</a> lets you practise it with short questions.</p>\n<p><strong>Philosophy and today’s problems.</strong> Philosophy is not just a legacy from the past: the new questions raised by science and technology are often philosophical questions. Four examples you will come across throughout the year:</p>\n<ul> <li><strong>Bioethics.</strong> In 2018 a Chinese researcher announced the birth of two baby girls whose embryos he had genetically modified; in 2021 Spain passed a law regulating euthanasia. How far can we modify human life? Who decides when to help someone die? You work on this in <a href=\"#teoria/fil-t5\">Topic 5</a>, in applied ethics.</li> <li><strong>Artificial intelligence.</strong> If a program writes a piece of homework, has it ‘understood’ anything? Who is answerable if an algorithm turns down a loan or a self-driving car runs someone over? These are questions about the mind (<a href=\"#teoria/fil-metafisica\">Topic M</a>, section 5) and about responsibility (<a href=\"#teoria/fil-t5\">Topic 5</a>).</li> <li><strong>Climate.</strong> Do we have duties to people who have not yet been born? Should those who have polluted most pay more? In 1979 Hans Jonas proposed an ethics of responsibility towards future generations. You will see it in <a href=\"#teoria/fil-t3\">Topic 3</a> and, in Year 2, in <a href=\"#teoria/hf-siglo21\">the challenges of the 21st century</a>.</li> <li><strong>Disinformation.</strong> When a hoax spreads faster than its denial, the question is philosophical: what is truth, whom do we believe and why? It is in <a href=\"#teoria/fil-t3\">Topic 3</a>, ‘Post-truth and <em>fake news</em>’.</li> </ul>\n<p><strong>Seeing the whole.</strong> Each of those problems cuts across several specialisms: climate touches physics, economics, law and ethics all at once. The specialist knows a great deal about one part; someone has to ask how the parts fit together and what ends we are pursuing with all of them. It is another way of understanding why philosophy is <strong>radical</strong> and <strong>universal</strong> (section 4). But to have an informed opinion on gene editing you need to know something about genetics: critical thinking does not work in a vacuum, and philosophy also obliges you to study the subject under discussion in depth.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: choose a news story from this week and look in it for a question that no data can settle. Which branch of philosophy would you put it in (section 5)?</p>\n<h2>9. The method of philosophy: reading and commenting on texts</h2>\n<p>Philosophising does not consist in repeating opinions, but in <strong>arguing</strong>: giving reasons, listening to those of others and examining them carefully. That is why a basic tool of this subject is the <strong>reading and commentary of texts</strong>. To comment on a philosophical text it is useful to follow a few simple steps:</p>\n<ul> <li><strong>Subject</strong>: what is the text about, in a single sentence?</li> <li><strong>Thesis</strong>: what main idea does the author defend?</li> <li><strong>Arguments</strong>: with what reasons does the author support that thesis? Distinguish the <strong>premises</strong> from the <strong>conclusion</strong> and ask yourself whether the conclusion follows from them (you will see this in <a href=\"#teoria/fil-t4\">Topic 4</a> and in the <a href=\"#logica\">Logic Corner</a>).</li> <li><strong>Reasoned opinion</strong>: do you agree? Give at least one reason, for or against.</li> </ul>\n<p>To this is added <strong>argumentative dialogue</strong>: in class, defending an idea does not consist in imposing it or shouting louder, but in supporting it with arguments and respecting the turns and the reasons of others. That is, at bottom, the philosophical attitude that runs through the whole topic: seeking the truth together by means of reason, without ever giving up the wonder that set it in motion.</p>\n<p>The other great written tool of the subject is the <strong>essay</strong>: a short text (in Year 1, 200 to 300 words) in which you answer a philosophical question. It has five parts: an <strong>introduction</strong> that poses the question and explains why it matters; two <strong>positions</strong> from authors on the syllabus that clash with each other, each with its reasons; your <strong>opinion</strong>, which takes sides with arguments of your own; and a <strong>conclusion</strong> that gives your answer in one or two sentences, without opening new topics. It is neither a summary of the topic nor a list of authors. The full guide, with the rubric and the typical faults, is in <a href=\"#disertaciones/fil-metodologia\">Essays</a>, together with a model essay for each topic (the one for this topic is <a href=\"#disertaciones/fil-modelo-filosofia\">‘Is philosophy of any use today?’</a>) and a <a href=\"#disertaciones/fil-temas\">list of questions to practise with</a>. The guide ends with an essay written with mistakes on purpose, so that you can correct it with the rubric in hand.</p>\n<p>A third tool, very characteristic of philosophy, is the <strong>thought experiment</strong>: imagining a situation, often impossible to carry out, in order to test a concept or a principle. Nothing is measured; what we observe is what we would say and why. Galileo used one against Aristotle: if heavy bodies fall faster, then by tying a large stone to a small one the small one would slow the large one down; but the pair, being heavier, ought to fall faster. The contradiction shows that the hypothesis fails (<em>Discourses on Two New Sciences</em>, 1638). The method consists in <strong>changing a single detail</strong> and seeing whether the answer changes. In the trolley case, diverting the vehicle with a lever or pushing a person off a bridge saves the same five and sacrifices one, and yet hardly anyone judges the two acts in the same way: something more than the number of lives weighs in our judgement. They have a limit, and Daniel Dennett gave it a name: they are ‘intuition pumps’, because a detail of the story that seems innocent can push us towards an answer without our noticing. That is why we must always ask whether the intuition holds when the case is told in a different way.</p>\n<p>For practice, the Dilemmas section has several classic thought experiments: <a href=\"#dilemas/fil-tranvia\">the trolley and the bridge</a>, <a href=\"#dilemas/fil-cubeta\">the brain in a vat</a>, <a href=\"#dilemas/fil-teseo\">the Ship of Theseus</a>, <a href=\"#dilemas/fil-fision\">the two who wake up</a>, <a href=\"#dilemas/fil-maquina\">the experience machine</a> and <a href=\"#dilemas/fil-velo\">the veil of ignorance</a>.</p>\n<h3>The rules of argumentative dialogue</h3>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t1-100\">The rules of a good dialogue are not rules of politeness but of method: they are there so that the best reason wins, and not whoever shouts loudest or holds the most power. Jürgen Habermas formulated them as the conditions of an <strong>ideal speech situation</strong>, taking up rules from the jurist Robert Alexy (<em>Moral Consciousness and Communicative Action</em>, 1983): anyone who is able to speak and act may take part; anyone may question any assertion, introduce a new one and express what they think and what they need; and no one may be prevented, by force or by pressure, from using these rights. Within that framework only one legitimate force remains, what Habermas called ‘the unforced force of the better argument’. Brought into the classroom, this comes down to a few rules:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Everyone with the same right to speak and to ask</strong>: no one has the last word because of their position, their fame or their marks.</li><li><strong>Everything can be discussed</strong>, including what the textbook or the teacher says, as long as reasons are given.</li><li><strong>No pressure</strong>: threats, mockery and ‘if you don’t think like me, it’s because…’ do not count.</li><li><strong>Sincerity</strong>: you defend what you really think, not what suits you in order to win.</li><li><strong>Being ready to change your mind</strong>: if the other person gives a better reason, you acknowledge it. Socrates already said that he preferred being refuted to refuting (you will see this in section 21 of <a href=\"#teoria/fil-t4\">Topic 4</a>).</li></ul>\n<p>The other piece is the art of <strong>questioning</strong>, which Socrates practised in Plato’s dialogues with a few moves that keep coming back. First he asks for a <strong>general definition</strong>: with Euthyphro, hearing examples of pious acts is not enough; he wants to know what makes all of them pious (<em>Euthyphro</em> 6d-e). Then he tests it with a <strong>case</strong>: when Laches defines courage as standing at one’s post without running away, Socrates reminds him of the soldiers who win by retreating and then attacking again (<em>Laches</em> 190e-191c). Next he shows that the definition clashes with something else the other person also accepts, and in the end both admit that they do not know, the <strong>aporia</strong>, and start again. Questioning like this is not humiliating the other person: it is examining an idea together. To practise it you have the <a href=\"#duelo\">Duel of Reasons</a>, and in the exploration on <a href=\"#teoria/fil-t1-lipman\">Matthew Lipman</a> you will see how a class becomes a community of inquiry.</p>\n<p>Another list, a more personal one, was written by Bertrand Russell in 1951: a ‘liberal decalogue’, ten pieces of advice for anyone who wants to think without fanaticism. Among them are never feeling absolutely certain of anything; not hiding the evidence that contradicts us, because it will come out in the end; answering whoever disagrees with us by giving them reasons, not by imposing our position on them; and not being afraid to think differently, since every idea we now accept seemed odd in its day. Habermas’s rules put the conversation in order; Russell’s advice, the character of the person taking part in it. Both are needed: a dialogue with flawless rules is no use if those who speak are not ready to doubt themselves.</p>\n<h3>Some methods of philosophy</h3>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t1-105\">Philosophy does not have a single method, as geometry does. Each period has forged its own, and you will use many of them during the year. These are the best known:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>What it does</th><th>Where it comes from</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Maieutics and refutation</td><td>Asking questions until a definition shows its flaws and a better one has to be found</td><td>Socrates, in Plato’s dialogues</td></tr><tr><td>Methodical doubt</td><td>Suspending everything that admits the slightest doubt, to see whether anything certain remains</td><td>Descartes, <em>Meditations on First Philosophy</em> (1641); in <a href=\"#teoria/fil-t3\">Topic 3</a></td></tr><tr><td>Geometric method</td><td>Starting from definitions and axioms and proving from them, as in Euclid</td><td>Spinoza, <em>Ethics</em> (1677); in the <a href=\"#teoria/fil-spinoza-sistema\">exploration on Spinoza</a></td></tr><tr><td>Transcendental method</td><td>Starting from something we already have (experience, science) and asking what makes it possible</td><td>Kant, <em>Critique of Pure Reason</em> (1781)</td></tr><tr><td>Phenomenological method</td><td>Describing how something presents itself to consciousness, bracketing whether or not it exists (<em>epoché</em>)</td><td>Husserl, <em>Ideas</em> I (1913)</td></tr><tr><td>Pragmatic maxim</td><td>Clarifying an idea by asking what practical effects its being true would have</td><td>Charles S. Peirce, ‘How to Make Our Ideas Clear’ (1878)</td></tr><tr><td>Falsification</td><td>Deliberately looking for what could refute a hypothesis</td><td>Popper, <em>Logik der Forschung</em> (1934); in Topics 3 and 4</td></tr><tr><td>Conceptual analysis</td><td>Proposing the conditions of a concept and testing them with counterexamples</td><td>Twentieth-century analytic philosophy; in section 22 of <a href=\"#teoria/fil-t4\">Topic 4</a></td></tr><tr><td>Thought experiment</td><td>Imagining a case to see what we would say about it</td><td>From Galileo to the present day; in this section</td></tr><tr><td>Reflective equilibrium</td><td>Adjusting principles and judgements about cases until they fit together</td><td>John Rawls, <em>A Theory of Justice</em> (1971); below</td></tr><tr><td>Experimental philosophy</td><td>Checking with surveys and experiments whether people share philosophers’ intuitions</td><td>Joshua Knobe, Shaun Nichols and others, since 2001; below</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Reflective equilibrium</strong> deserves a separate explanation, because it is the method you will use most in ethics without knowing that you are using it. We have <strong>general principles</strong> (‘one must not lie’) and <strong>judgements about cases</strong> of which we are very sure (‘lying to the murderer who asks where your friend is hiding is right’). When they clash, something has to be corrected: either the principle, which was perhaps too crude, or the judgement, which was perhaps a prejudice. We go back and forth between the two until they hold together. The case of the murderer is not made up: Benjamin Constant and Immanuel Kant argued about it in 1797, and Kant kept to the principle. Rawls gave the method its name (<em>A Theory of Justice</em>, 1971, §§ 4 and 9), following Nelson Goodman, who had used it to justify the rules of induction. The equilibrium is never final: a new case can break it. And its weak point is clear: if all the starting judgements were biased, the method only puts them in order.</p>\n<p>Both thought experiments and reflective equilibrium rest on <strong>intuitions</strong>: what we think we would say about a case. Since the beginning of the twenty-first century, <strong>experimental philosophy</strong> has been asking whether these intuitions are as widely shared as philosophers assume, and it checks this with surveys and experiments. A well-known result is the Knobe effect (2003). Someone who reads that the head of a company approved a plan knowing that it would harm the environment, although he only cared about profits, thinks that he harmed it ‘intentionally’; if the plan improved the environment, hardly anyone says that he improved it intentionally. The moral judgement slips into a concept that seemed merely descriptive. Its critics reply that a survey collects quick reactions, not considered intuitions, and that philosophy does not consist in counting votes. The debate remains open, and it is itself a question of method.</p>\n<h3>Reviewing what you write: the curse of knowledge</h3>\n<p>Someone who knows something finds it very hard to imagine how it looks to someone who does not. Some economists called this the <strong>curse of knowledge</strong> (Camerer, Loewenstein and Weber, 1989), and it shows in any explanation: ‘it’s the second on the right, past the bar’ only helps if you already know the bar. The same thing happens in an essay. Because you know what you mean, your text seems clear to you even if steps are missing, and you do not see the <strong>implicit premises</strong> that the reader would have to guess (section 20 of <a href=\"#teoria/fil-t4\">Topic 4</a>). To review it you have to change places: read the text as if you had not been in class; check that every technical term is explained and that every reason rests on something that is said, not on something the reader does not have in front of them; and ask someone to sum up your thesis in their own words. If their summary does not match what you meant, the problem is in the text, not in the reader.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: if in a dialogue no one can impose themselves by force and everyone can ask about everything, do we always reach agreement? And if we do not, has the dialogue failed?</p>\n<h2>10. Sample exam: recognise the branch</h2> <p>To finish, a small <strong>sample exam</strong> like the one you will do in class. It does not measure how much you memorise, but whether you know <strong>which branch each problem lives in</strong>. Revise section 5 first, and the deck <a href=\"#tarjetas/fil-ramas\">‘The branches of philosophy’</a>.</p> <p><strong>1.</strong> Say which <strong>branch</strong> of philosophy each question belongs to:</p> <ul> <li>(a) How would you convince someone that the Sun does not ‘rise’ over the horizon?</li> <li>(b) What is the root of evil in the human being?</li> <li>(c) What does it mean to <em>be</em> or to <em>exist</em>?</li> <li>(d) Is there anything universally beautiful?</li> <li>(e) How can you show that someone is contradicting themselves?</li> <li>(f) Why does choosing fill us with anguish? Is it possible not to choose?</li> <li>(g) What is a <em>utopia</em> and how can a just society be organised?</li> </ul> <p><strong>2.</strong> Choose two of the questions above and try to <strong>answer them</strong> briefly, in your own words.</p> <p><strong>3.</strong> Your teacher will give you a <strong>short text</strong>: read it, say which branch it belongs to and justify why.</p> <p class=\"aporia\">To think about: one and the same person can speak from different branches. When Aristotle states that ‘moral excellence is acquired as a habit’ he is doing <strong>ethics</strong>; when he states the principle of non-contradiction he is doing <strong>logic</strong>. Do not classify by the <em>author</em>, but by the type of <em>question</em>.</p> <p><strong>Trick case:</strong> ‘Is it true that the Earth goes round the Sun?’ looks like, and is, a question of <strong>science</strong>. But ‘what is truth?’ or ‘how do I know my senses are not deceiving me?’ are no longer settled by any experiment: they belong to <strong>epistemology</strong>. The branch is not decided by the subject being talked about, but by the <strong>type of question</strong> that is asked about it.</p>"}});
